api_sketch:
Types (high-level):

interface StreamConnector<T> {
  toAsyncIterable(): AsyncIterable<T>;
  toWebStream(): ReadableStream<T>;
  tee(): [StreamConnector<T>, StreamConnector<T>];
  transform<U>(fn: (chunk: T) => U | Promise<U>): StreamConnector<U>;
  retryWithCheckpoint(opts: {maxAttempts:number, backoffMs?:number}): StreamConnector<T>;
}

Protocol notes:
- Checkpoint tokens: sources may yield special metadata chunks of shape {__checkpoint: string} that connectors treat opaquely and surface to retry helpers.
- FlowControlToken: when converting to WebStream, the producer receives an awaitable token the connector resolves when downstream is ready. This preserves backpressure when bridging between event-driven producers and pull-based consumers.

example_snippets:
// Basic conversion: ReadableStream -> AsyncIterator
const connector = StreamConnector.fromWebStream(response.body as ReadableStream<Uint8Array>);
for await (const chunk of connector.toAsyncIterable()) {
  // chunk is Uint8Array or decoded string depending on transform
  processChunk(chunk);
}

// Tee to two consumers (logger + main consumer)
const [logConn, mainConn] = connector.tee();
void (async () => { for await (const c of logConn.toAsyncIterable()) console.debug('stream:', c); })();
for await (const c of mainConn.toAsyncIterable()) handle(c);

// Retry with checkpoint: connector will surface last checkpoint and resume
await connector.retryWithCheckpoint({maxAttempts:3}).toAsyncIterable()
  .forEach(chunk => applyToModelState(chunk));

// Flow-control example bridging a callback producer to WebStream consumer
const connector2 = StreamConnector.fromCallbackProducer(async (emit) => {
  for (let i=0;i<100;i++) {
    await emit({data: `token-${i}`}); // emit waits for backpressure token when needed
  }
});
const stream = connector2.toWebStream();
// stream can be consumed in browser fetch response pipeline
